Bullous Pemphigoid (Mild Localised)
Autoimmune Conditions Mild Severity
Few scattered tense bullae, often on lower legs, minimal pruritus

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AMS-007
Reason for Visit: Acute eruption of unexplained skin blisters on the lower extremities requiring exclusion of infectious cellulitis.
Location: Nursing home
Present at Visit: Patient and facility nursing staff
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated urgent assessment was required to evaluate the sudden onset of lower limb bullae in an elderly resident. Immediate differentiation from acute bullous cellulitis or severe allergic reaction was required to prevent rapid systemic deterioration and determine the need for intravenous antibiotics prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My legs have these large water blisters on them that popped up today, and the nurses are worried it's a severe infection.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports noticing atypical localized blistering in older adults starting approximately 12 hours ago. The lesions began as small erythematous patches on the bilateral lower legs and rapidly evolved into tense, fluid-filled blisters. There is mild localized tightness but no severe pain, radiating heat, or significant pruritus. The abrupt presentation caused facility staff to isolate the patient pending medical review.
Functional Status: Mobility-impaired, utilizes a walker.
Social/Safety Concerns: Risk of secondary bacterial skin infection if blisters rupture.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/75 | HR: 82 | Temp: 36.9 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 96% | Wt: 64 Kg
General: Alert, comfortable at rest, not displaying systemic toxicity.
Physical Exam: Dermatological examination reveals a few scattered tense bullae, often on lower legs, minimal pruritus. The surrounding skin shows mild macular erythema but lacks intense warmth, induration, or ascending lymphangitis. Bullae contain clear serous fluid. Nikolsky sign is negative. Peripheral pulses are palpable.
Environmental Observations: Facility room is well-maintained. Bedrails are padded to prevent inadvertent skin trauma.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against facility chart. No recent commencement of new medications (e.g., specific antibiotics or diuretics known to trigger bullae).

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Bullous Pemphigoid (Mild Localised) - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Bullous Cellulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Afebrile status, absence of spreading warmth, significant pain, or systemic signs of sepsis.

Stasis Dermatitis with Blistering - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of significant chronic venous edema, hemosiderin staining, or weeping eczema.

Pemphigus Vulgaris - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Tense (rather than flaccid) nature of the bullae, negative Nikolsky sign, and lack of mucosal involvement.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (frailty and risk of secondary infection)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with an acute eruption of tense bullae on the lower limbs, a clinical picture highly suspicious for bullous cellulitis in the residential aged care setting. Triage appropriately escalated the call to prevent delayed antibiotic administration in a vulnerable host. Clinical examination conclusively ruled out acute soft tissue infection, identifying the lesions as a classic localized autoimmune blistering process. Timely assessment prevented an unnecessary hospital transfer, avoided the inappropriate use of broad-spectrum antibiotics, and permitted the safe initiation of topical therapy, fulfilling the criteria for urgent after-hours intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Fluid aspirated from the largest bulla with a sterile needle to relieve tension; blister roof left intact to act as a biological dressing. Prescribed high-potency topical corticosteroid ointment.
Positive Behavioral Change: Apply the prescribed ointment precisely to the red areas around the blisters and keep your legs slightly elevated while seated. Diligent application and elevation will actively shrink the blisters and reduce skin tension, allowing you to walk with less discomfort.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Directed facility nursing staff to perform daily skin checks.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3 days to monitor response to topical steroids and arrange biopsy if needed.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the blisters rapidly multiply, the surrounding skin becomes severely painful, red, and hot, or if the patient develops a fever, facility staff must arrange immediate transfer to the Emergency Department.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending formal dermatological biopsy; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: Localized bullous pemphigoid typically responds well to topical steroids, though lesions may take weeks to heal completely.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Notify the nursing staff immediately if the fluid in the blisters turns cloudy or yellow, or if you feel sudden chills and fever.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your immune system is mistakenly attacking the layer between your skin layers, causing fluid to pool. The steroid ointment will calm this local reaction.
Actionable Steps: Avoid scratching or intentionally popping the blisters, as the skin covering them is the best protection against infection. Keep your legs elevated.

SMS INFORMATION

Dx: Localised Bullous Pemphigoid. No signs of infection. High-strength steroid ointment prescribed. Facility nurses to monitor daily. See GP in 3 days.

SMS EDUCATION

Do not scratch or peel the blisters. Keep legs elevated. Report any cloudy blister fluid, severe pain, or fever immediately.
